// std.verify — named proof bundles over the verification atoms
// (docs/design/rfc-properties.md slice 2).
//
// A `property` is a named, parameterized conjunction of proof atoms —
// invariant clauses (single- or two-state) and guarded_by clauses. A
// `satisfies` clause on a class or object instantiates the bundle by pure
// substitution, and the compiler discharges the instantiated atoms exactly
// as if they were hand-written; the property NAME survives as the type's
// exported fact. The compiler knows nothing about monotonicity or fencing —
// it knows how to check the atoms these bodies name (epistemics.md,
// "Properties are someone's responsibility").

// The field never decreases. A two-state invariant proven at every
// `mut self` method boundary (relative to the method's entry state) and at
// every foreign write site (relative to the pre-write state). Monotone
// facts are the invalidation-free corner of the warrant gradient
// (docs/design/epistemics.md): once true, never false — safe to cache and
// share at any distance.
pub property monotonic(f: field<int>) {
    invariant f >= old(f)
}

// Fencing: no write to `f` escapes the authority's check. Every write site
// of the instantiated field must be dominated, in its method's control
// flow, by a conditional the prover can carry to the write, showing that
// some in-scope grant's `token` equals the current authority value. The
// grant type must be a concrete value class carrying a `token: int` field;
// the authority is an int field of the carrying type. Pairs with
// `monotonic(authority)`: a fence is only sound if a minted token can
// never become current again.
pub property fenced(f: field, authority: field<int>, grant: type) {
    f guarded_by (g: grant) g.token == authority
}
